No, maybe it's not a hand. We're on the beach. It's on the beach.

1737
01:54:38.254 --> 01:54:41.274
There's somebody trying to work out if they should be putting their hand up or not.

1738
01:54:41.284 --> 01:54:43.514
They're drowning. They're losing control.

1739
01:54:43.523 --> 01:54:47.014
Do we want the current to take them to the ambulance at the bottom of the cliff

1740
01:54:47.054 --> 01:54:48.714
before they've got that clemency?

1741
01:54:48.754 --> 01:54:49.274
Or could we

1742
01:54:50.154 --> 01:54:53.854
maybe help more people if we made an amendment and just

1743
01:54:53.914 --> 01:54:57.734
expanded that grace and clemency to people who have

1744
01:54:57.814 --> 01:55:01.794
put up their hand and asked for help? This is what I'm asking for and suggesting.

1745
01:55:01.854 --> 01:55:05.824
How about an amendment that just opens that window for people that

1746
01:55:05.874 --> 01:55:09.634
are engaging with services now to give them an

1747
01:55:09.694 --> 01:55:11.954
incentive to engage? Because

1748
01:55:12.994 --> 01:55:16.934
if you look at treatment, you look at intervention, all the journals

1749
01:55:16.974 --> 01:55:20.924
will tell you that early intervention is not only a more humane way to

1750
01:55:20.934 --> 01:55:24.034
treat people, but it's more cost-effective. Let's think about it.

1751
01:55:24.314 --> 01:55:27.974
All we need to do is to throw him a rope. He doesn't need the ambulance yet.

1752
01:55:28.034 --> 01:55:31.634
He just needs someone to talk to. Because Gabor Maté, one of the

1753
01:55:31.694 --> 01:55:35.644
leaders in addiction understanding, tells us that addiction, it's

1754
01:55:35.674 --> 01:55:39.484
not an illness, it's a symptom. It's a symptom of

1755
01:55:39.534 --> 01:55:39.954
trauma.

1756
01:55:41.034 --> 01:55:42.874
Trauma doesn't heal in isolation.

1757
01:55:42.934 --> 01:55:45.574
I'll tell you, when I was an addict, when I've been through those times, and others

1758
01:55:45.634 --> 01:55:49.614
as well, the feelings of the drug user, the addict, are isolation.

1759
01:55:49.654 --> 01:55:53.474
The feeling that we're not understood, that I'm addicted to this stuff because it's

1760
01:55:53.494 --> 01:55:55.374
the same as the medicines I had as a child.

1761
01:55:55.414 --> 01:55:57.064
And when I go and ask for help, I don't get any.

1762
01:55:57.394 --> 01:56:00.654
And the government are just trying to punish me. I'm the droid they're looking for.

1763
01:56:01.104 --> 01:56:04.654
And so that's the feeling. It's being cut off from society, ostracized, and pushed

1764
01:56:04.714 --> 01:56:06.894
out. And the way to get past that is

1765
01:56:07.754 --> 01:56:10.874
to incentivize engagement and connection.

1766
01:56:11.094 --> 01:56:13.194
How can we get people to put up their hand and ask?

1767
01:56:13.614 --> 01:56:17.454
It's to take away the fear and incentivize. So that's what I'm asking for.

1768
01:56:18.134 --> 01:56:18.814
Back to the beach

1769
01:56:21.710 --> 01:56:25.370
So yeah, my feeling is that if we were to, rather than just

1770
01:56:25.430 --> 01:56:25.870
making the

1771
01:56:26.810 --> 01:56:30.670
emergency ambulance at the bottom of the cliff a safe space, let's see if we

1772
01:56:30.710 --> 01:56:34.070
can create more safe spaces for people to engage services.

1773
01:56:35.530 --> 01:56:37.170
Because trauma heals in connection.

1774
01:56:38.370 --> 01:56:42.350
I think that's basically the guts of what I wanted to say.

1775
01:56:42.510 --> 01:56:42.560
Thank you very much.

1776
01:56:42.570 --> 01:56:45.590
We did a clinical trial years ago for a drug called Ease, which you never heard

1777
01:56:45.630 --> 01:56:47.450
about because I didn't put it in the theories. I'd learned.

1778
01:56:47.830 --> 01:56:47.840
That's all.

1779
01:56:47.840 --> 01:56:49.150
And so my time's up?

1780
01:56:49.250 --> 01:56:53.110
You are over time. So I'll let you

1781
01:56:53.170 --> 01:56:54.170
come to a conclusion.

1782
01:56:54.990 --> 01:56:58.760
We set up a system where anybody wants to get the drugs, they come into

1783
01:56:58.790 --> 01:57:01.770
relationship with a clinician first. We monitor the usage patterns.

1784
01:57:01.810 --> 01:57:05.670
As soon as there's a spike, we get a hold of them and say, "Hey, why has your drug

1785
01:57:05.810 --> 01:57:09.650
usage increased? Have you had a relationship breakup, or have you lost your

1786
01:57:09.690 --> 01:57:13.110
job?" There's one of two or three reasons normally why that happens, and we were

1787
01:57:13.190 --> 01:57:16.620
able to early intervention, just like that, we were able to get much better

1788
01:57:16.670 --> 01:57:19.550
outcomes. And that's the way I'd like to see us move forwards.

1789
01:57:20.710 --> 01:57:22.590
Thank you very much for your presentation.

1790
01:57:22.730 --> 01:57:22.970
Okay.

1791
01:57:23.230 --> 01:57:24.090
It's good to have you here.

1792
01:57:24.470 --> 01:57:25.810
Okay. Thanks, guys.

1793
01:57:26.150 --> 01:57:26.420
Thank you.

1794
01:57:26.420 --> 01:57:26.510
Thank you.

